From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Flavius <Flavius256@icloud.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 07:06:30 +0000
Subject: named addresses

Name globals and functions instead of literal addresses.

The port relocates a global or function to each level's copy by its name. A literal address,
or one from the PAL decompilation, is read as is and lands in the wrong copy.

- FUN_L00_00276368: the pause menu mobys' update is stored as FUN_0023a2c0 by name, not as the
  literal 0x2DF638. The literal is level 0's place of that function; each level's program forms
  its own. In any other level the mobys' update went to an address no function has, and with it
  the pause page frames' animations.
- render_capture_effect_pass (FUN_00238630): the salesman flag is read as D_001610A8[0]
  (declared in the file already), not through the literal *(s32 *)0x1610A8. The port relocates a
  global to the loaded level's copy by its name; a literal address is read as is, so in a level
  the vendor's flag came from the executable's copy, which is never 1, and the salesman was
  never drawn into his screen. The expected assembly forms the same lui $3, %hi(D_001610A8) / lw
  $3, %lo(D_001610A8)($3)
  (build/baseline/config/us/expected/asm/gameplay/vendor/fun_00238630/func_00238630.s; retail
  0x238658). Not rebuilt.
- FUN_L08_002284e0 (level 8's hero movement update): the run-speed threshold read as
  D_L08_0017C5B8.x0C (the struct the file declares), not through D_L08_0017C5C4[] labelled
  __asm__("D_L08_0017C5B8+12"). Hostgen reads a label as a symbol name and knows none called
  D_L08_0017C5B8+12, so the whole function became a stub: on Batalia Ratchet took the walk state
  but never moved or turned. The retail load is lwc1 $f0, -0x3A3C($2) with $2 = 0x180000, i.e.
  0x17C5C4 = 0x17C5B8 + 0xC (0x22C9D8). Same address, same instructions; not rebuilt.
- FUN_L07_002f8058 (an ambient-sound moby's update, Umbris and Gaspar): its draw callback is
  labelled FUN_L07_002f8038, not FUN_L07_0030d420. The retail lui/addiu at 0x2F80B0 forms
  0x2F8038 (build/overlay-asm/level_07/split/f002F8058/FUN_L07_002f8058.s), the wrapper that
  runs the ambient table D_L07_001D3400 the update sets up; 0x30D420 is the ocean's
  (taxi_platform.c, D_L07_00208560). The port forms each level's copy of the address from the
  copy's own instructions, which form no 0x30D420, so it fell back to a relocated place: on
  Gaspar (copy at 0x2EF7F8, retail forms FUN_L09_002ef750) that was the level's copy of the pad
  update, and the pad was read twice a tick (every press's first-frame edge lost: a jump pressed
  during a knockback was never taken). Same address in level 7; not rebuilt.
- nanotech_upgrade_vendor_update (FUN_L10_002e8358): the bolts are taken from current_bolt_count
  (D_0015ED98), the new maximum goes to hero_max_health (D_0015EDA0), and the timer set after
  the save message is dialogue_message_duration (D_L10_0015F640). The port from PAL wrote
  D_0013ED98, D_0013EDA0 and D_L10_0013F640, 0x20000 below the US globals (PAL gBolts
  D_0015EE98, hero_max_health D_0015EEA0); the retail code
  (config/us/overlays/asm/FUN_L10_002e8358.s) reads and writes D_0015ED98, D_0015EDA0 and
  D_L10_0015F640, as Orxon's magnetboots pickup (platform.c) sets the same timer after its
  message. Translated, the Orxon nanotech vendor set the bolts to an unrelated word less 4000 (a
  player saw -3), the maximum health was not raised, and the timer's 0xB4 went into another
  unrelated word. Not rebuilt.

